  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1... \nA dominant upper level ridge will remain over the region through\nTuesday allowing for dry and pleasant conditions to continue.\nSunny skies today will give way to some high clouds building\nacross the area tomorrow. High temperatures today will climb\ninto the mid to upper 70s before warming a bit on Tuesday with\nincreased WAA into the low to mid 80s. Overnight lows tonight\nwill drop into the mid to upper 50s before remaining more mild\nin the upper 60s to low 70s Tuesday night. In addition to\nincreased WAA over the area, a shift to southwest flow ahead of\nan approaching cold front will increase dewpoints on Tuesday,\nresulting in more humid conditions returning to the region. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... \nUnsettled weather returns to the area on Wednesday as a cold\nfront associated with a low over Canada is expected to push east\nacross the area. An strengthening LLJ coupled with favorable\ndiurnal timing of showers should aid in shower and storm\ndevelopment across the area Wednesday afternoon through the\nevening hours. Increasing PWAT values coupled with a deep warm\nlayer will also present the potential for locally heavy rainfall\nand subsequent isolated flooding concerns. Models are still\ndiverging on the exact timing which would play into the severe\npotential, but at this point both SPC and WPC maintain a \nMarginal Risk (1 of 5) across much of the area. \n\nAside from the severe potential, winds are expected to be gusty\non Wednesday from the southwest with sustained winds of 10-20\nmph and gusts up to 30 mph possible. Areas east of I71 are most\nlikely to receive these gusty winds. High temperatures for the\nweek will peak on Wednesday as they climb into the mid to upper\n80s. Overnight lows will linger in the mid to upper 60s.",
